## `routes/orgWalletRoutes.js` — **PATCH & KEEP** (don't create from scratch, it exists)

Three problems against the rules:

### Problem 1 — Wrong policy on GET routes (violates Rule C)
> "only an org admin can access the org wallet"

`organization.view` is currently defined as:

```js
'organization.view': (actor, org) => {
  if (!org) return false;
  if (!actor) return org.isPublic === true;
  if (actor.isPlatformAdmin) return true;
  if (actor.activeOrgId?.equals(org._id)) return true;   // ← ANY member
  return org.isPublic === true;
}
```

That means **any** member (receptionist, doctor, staff) of the org — not just admins — can currently `GET /orgs/:orgId/wallet` and `/entries`. This leaks wallet balance and full financial history to non-admins.

**Fix:** use `subscription.manage` on all three routes so read + write are locked to admins consistently:

```js
router.get  ('/',        authenticate, authorize('subscription.manage', loadOrg), walletController.getOrgWallet);
router.post ('/topup',   authenticate, authorize('subscription.manage', loadOrg), paymentController.orgWalletTopup);
router.get  ('/entries', authenticate, authorize('subscription.manage', loadOrg), walletController.getOrgEntries);
```

(If you dislike the name `subscription.manage` covering wallets, rename it to something neutral like `org.finance` — but do not use `organization.view` here.)

### Problem 2 — `/topup` points at the wrong handler (violates Rule C in a worse way)

It's wired to `walletController.topUpOrg`, which does this:

```js
async topUpOrg(req, res) {
  const wallet = await walletService.orgTopUp({ orgId: req.params.orgId, amount });
  ...
}
```

That's a **direct credit** to the org wallet with no money movement — an admin could conjure balance out of thin air. Rule C says top-ups must go through Paymob.

**Fix:** point it at `paymentController.orgWalletTopup` (the Paymob-intention flow you already have in `paymentController`) and **delete** `walletController.topUpOrg` + `walletService.orgTopUp` from the *public API*. Keep `walletService.orgTopUp` only as an internal function called from `paymentService.handleWebhook` on the `org_wallet_topup` success case.

Rename the internal function to make the boundary obvious, e.g. `walletService._creditOrgFromPaymob(orgId, amount, paymentId)` — anything to signal "not callable from a controller".

### Problem 3 — Duplicate route with `walletRoutes.js`

`walletRoutes.js` also has:

```js
router.post('/:orgId/topup', authenticate, authorize('organization.manage', loadOrg), paymentController.orgWalletTopup);
```

Two different mount paths (`/wallet/:orgId/topup` and `/orgs/:orgId/wallet/topup`) do the same thing, with **two different policies** (`organization.manage` vs `subscription.manage`). Keep only the nested one under `orgWalletRoutes.js` and delete `walletRoutes.js` entirely as I recommended.

